  "description": "'Done, vetoed nothing' (runoff_submitted_at set, no Veto row) is recorded explicitly and is not a veto; 'hasn't opened the app' (runoff_submitted_at null) is a distinct third state that blocks nothing",
  "spec_clause": "Cross-cutting rules: 'Record veto-pass skips explicitly for the same reason: done, vetoed nothing is not hasn't opened the app.' + Data model Attendance: 'null = hasn't opened the app / set = done, even if they vetoed nothing'",
  "rationale": "3 attendees, two finalists. m1: u1,u2 yes / u3 no -> attendee_votes 3, coverage 1.00, yes 2, approval 0.6667. m2: u1,u3 yes / u2 no -> yes 2, approval 0.6667. Both promoted (tie on yes-votes, not at the boundary, so only the set is asserted). u1 and u2 have runoff_submitted_at set with no Veto row = explicit passes; u3 has runoff_submitted_at null = never opened the app. There are zero Veto rows, so veto counts are 0 for both movies and nothing is disqualified - an explicit pass must never be read as a veto of anything, and a missing submission must never be read as a veto either, nor block the round from closing. Pairwise: u1 and u2 both prefer m1 = 2, m2 = 0, u3 cast no pair vote at all (not_voted, contributes to neither side) -> m1 beats m2 = Condorcet winner.",
